---
import CookbookShell from './CookbookShell.astro';
---

<CookbookShell title={`Introduction | BiblicalMeal Cookbook`}>
  <section class="cookbook-content px-5 py-16 sm:px-8 sm:py-24">
    <h1 class="font-display text-h1 text-ink">Introduction</h1>

    <div class="prose-bm mt-8 max-w-2xl space-y-6 text-body text-ink-muted">
      <p>
        Every culture is shaped by what it eats. The foods of the biblical world
        &mdash; bread baked on hot stones, lentils stewed in clay pots, fish
        caught from Galilean waters, honey gathered from wild hives &mdash; are
        not just historical curiosities. They are the daily realities that
        nourished the people whose stories fill Scripture.
      </p>

      <p>
        This cookbook exists to bring those foods back to your kitchen. Not as
        museum pieces or academic exercises, but as real, cookable recipes that
        honour the evidence while acknowledging its limits. Every recipe here is
        built from three kinds of knowledge: what Scripture says, what history
        reveals, and what works in a modern kitchen today.
      </p>

      <p>
        We have been honest about what we know and what we don&rsquo;t. Some
        recipes are grounded in explicitly attested ingredients and historical
        preparation methods. Others are modern creations inspired by
        biblical-world ingredients where no specific ancient recipe exists. In
        every case, you will find clear labels telling you exactly what kind of
        recipe you are cooking and how confident the historical evidence is.
      </p>

      <p>
        That honesty is what makes this cookbook different. We don&rsquo;t
        present uncertain reconstructions as discovered facts. We don&rsquo;t
        pretend that a modern kitchen can perfectly replicate an ancient
        preparation. And we don&rsquo;t hide uncertainty behind confident
        language. Instead, we invite you to cook with us &mdash; knowing exactly
        where the evidence is strong, where it is reasonable, and where it runs
        out.
      </p>

      <p>
        The result is a collection of recipes that are genuinely cookable,
        historically grounded, and transparent about their evidence. Whether you
        are preparing a simple lentil pottage, baking barley bread, or
        assembling bitter greens for a Passover table, you are participating in
        a tradition that stretches back millennia.
      </p>

      <p>Welcome to Biblical Meals.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</CookbookShell>
